  "opening": {
    "en": [
      {
        "title": "Starter Deck Choice",
        "body": "The three starter decks (Pikachu/Mewtwo/Charizard focus) each represent a different archetype. Mewtwo EX Psychic provides the fastest win potential (Mewtwo EX attacks for 150 with 2 energy); Charizard offers heavy hitter tanking; Pikachu offers balance. For immediate ladder climbing, Mewtwo EX or Charizard EX archetypes have the highest win rates in early seasons. Invest Pack Points into supplementing the starter deck's weaknesses before opening packs for collection purposes."
      },
      {
        "title": "Early Collection Priority",
        "body": "Focus pack opening on a single booster set rather than splitting across sets—concentration maximises Pack Point accumulation for specific targets. The \"Genetic Apex\" set contains the highest-value competitive staples (Pikachu EX, Mewtwo EX, Charizard EX). Spend Pack Points on the highest-rarity version of your primary attacker first, then fill in supporter and trainer cards with common-rarity alternatives until higher-rarity equivalents become available."
      }
    ]
  },
  "midGame": {
    "en": [
      {
        "title": "Deck Consistency Optimisation",
        "body": "Consistency is more valuable than power in TCG Pocket—a deck that draws its key pieces by turn 3 consistently outperforms a more powerful deck that draws them by turn 5. Include 4× copies of your primary attacker (or the maximum allowed), 4× Professor's Research, and at least 2× Pokémon Communication. Thin the deck below 20 cards if allowed by game rules to increase draw probability; in TCG Pocket the 20-card format already maximises this effect compared to the standard 60-card format."
      },
      {
        "title": "Bench Management Strategy",
        "body": "Bench positions are limited to 3 in TCG Pocket—never fill all three bench slots simultaneously unless you need immediate energy acceleration. Keep one bench slot open as a \"buffer\" against bench-targeting moves (Mewtwo EX's Psydrive hits bench Pokémon). The optimal bench configuration: 1 energy-building future attacker + 1 flexible evolving basic + 1 empty slot. Overfilling the bench against specific meta decks (those with bench damage) costs 1–2 prize cards from avoidable bench damage."
      }
    ]
  },
  "endGame": {
    "en": [
      {
        "title": "Ranked Season Ladder Strategy",
        "body": "Ranked matches in TCG Pocket use a star-rating system where consistent top-8 finishes matter more than single tournament wins. Play the highest win-rate archetype for your card collection rather than attempting to force an incomplete deck build. Track the current meta (which archetypes appear most in ranked) and adjust your tech cards (usually the 2–4 flexible trainer slots) to counter the most common opponent strategy rather than over-optimising for your ideal setup."
      }
    ]
  },
